WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:01.960
Hey Thoughty2 here.

00:00:01.960 --> 00:00:07.709
The year is 1902, twelve young men at the
peak of their physical fitness and the peak

00:00:07.709 --> 00:00:12.610
of their careers are sat round a table, dressed
in their finest evening-wear.

00:00:12.610 --> 00:00:16.330
Silver cutlery and crisp white linen adorn
the table.

00:00:16.330 --> 00:00:21.930
They are served only the highest quality food,
cooked with the expertise of a top chef and

00:00:21.930 --> 00:00:25.330
a top toxicologist.

00:00:25.330 --> 00:00:30.449
Because every mouthful of this delicious food
has been laced with poison.

00:00:30.449 --> 00:00:35.540
From formaldehyde in the mashed potato to
borax in the lamb shank.

00:00:35.540 --> 00:00:40.770
Every tasty meal is laden with chemicals intended
to kill humans.

00:00:40.770 --> 00:00:47.329
But these men were very aware what they were
eating and did so willingly.

00:00:47.329 --> 00:00:49.510
For they were the Poison Squad.

00:00:49.510 --> 00:00:55.210
The willing participants of this macabre squad
were mostly medical students or employees

00:00:55.210 --> 00:00:57.610
of the Bureau of Chemistry.

00:00:57.610 --> 00:01:02.820
For five years the men sat down to a nightly
meal prepared by a government-run kitchen.

00:01:02.820 --> 00:01:09.540
They would be fed a range of toxic and poisonous
substances, mixed up and hidden within their

00:01:09.540 --> 00:01:10.540
meals.

00:01:10.540 --> 00:01:15.810
Each member of the squad was monitored meticulously
at all times to observe the effects.

00:01:15.810 --> 00:01:22.570
If a member started to get seriously ill from
a poison their dose would be reduced.

00:01:22.570 --> 00:01:28.350
The poison squad was a daring new experiment
by chemist Dr. Harvey Wiley.

00:01:28.350 --> 00:01:33.760
Its goal was to identify harmful substances
that were making their way into our food so

00:01:33.760 --> 00:01:35.560
food labelling could be improved.

00:01:35.560 --> 00:01:42.370
You see, up until this experiment, manufacturers
would chuck any old substance into food products

00:01:42.370 --> 00:01:50.470
to achieve the desired effects, such as strychnine
in Bitter Beer and arsenic in children's sweets.

00:01:50.470 --> 00:01:56.500
Something needed to be done and this daring
experiment played a large part in the creation

00:01:56.500 --> 00:01:59.430
of the Food and Drug Administration.

00:01:59.430 --> 00:02:05.560
The Year the experiment ended the Pure Food
and Drug act of 1906 was passed in the US.

00:02:05.560 --> 00:02:11.580
Sadly one member of the poison squad died
due to his organs eventually shutting down

00:02:11.580 --> 00:02:14.160
from all the ingested poison.

00:02:14.160 --> 00:02:19.700
Thankfully we've come a long way from having
to eat poisons to test our food products.

00:02:19.700 --> 00:02:26.530
Even though practically everything we eat
today is safe, we all know at least one "picky

00:02:26.530 --> 00:02:32.160
eater", that friend who will eat nothing but
fried chicken and then of course there's that

00:02:32.160 --> 00:02:39.220
guy you know who will eat absolutely anything,
from olives to deep-fried insects.

00:02:39.220 --> 00:02:42.330
Maybe the picky eater is you?

00:02:42.330 --> 00:02:47.810
But what separates these two people, we're
all born with the same equipment for tasting

00:02:47.810 --> 00:02:53.980
food so why do certain foods provoke reactions
of disgust, even hate, in some people?

00:02:53.980 --> 00:03:00.560
There's one particular animal that knows all
too well about picky eating, the rat.

00:03:00.560 --> 00:03:07.010
Rats are one of nature's pickiest animals,
for one good reason, they don't possess the

00:03:07.010 --> 00:03:09.650
ability to vomit.

00:03:09.650 --> 00:03:14.970
If a rat ingests something toxic and gets
food poisoning, it has no mechanism to rid

00:03:14.970 --> 00:03:20.250
the spoiled food from its body, which can
often be fatal.

00:03:20.250 --> 00:03:25.610
Rats have therefore evolved to be extremely
suspicious of new foods and will do something

00:03:25.610 --> 00:03:29.070
called "sampling" when they come across new
food.

00:03:29.070 --> 00:03:33.880
Rats will taste a tiny bit of the food to
see if it will make them sick, if not then

00:03:33.880 --> 00:03:37.540
it will return later and gobble the whole
lot.

00:03:37.540 --> 00:03:42.500
Fortunately, we humans have various mechanisms
to protect ourselves against food poisoning

00:03:42.500 --> 00:03:47.140
and we can often recover from a dodgy meal
without medical assistance.

00:03:47.140 --> 00:03:53.560
But just like the rat our pickiness about
certain foods comes from our evolutionary

00:03:53.560 --> 00:03:54.980
past.

00:03:54.980 --> 00:04:01.060
Scientists have studied why some people are
averse to some foods and they suspect it has

00:04:01.060 --> 00:04:04.990
to do with our time spent as hunter gatherers.

00:04:04.990 --> 00:04:10.000
Long before and even for many hundreds of
thousands of years after, our hominid ancestors

00:04:10.000 --> 00:04:16.639
invented the spear and other hunting tools,
we had to forage to survive.

00:04:16.639 --> 00:04:22.070
Without farming and because hunting meat was
unpredictable, our ancestors had to live off

00:04:22.070 --> 00:04:26.740
foraged nuts, seeds, berries and vegetation
to survive.

00:04:26.740 --> 00:04:33.200
But many berries, nuts and plants contain
deadly poisons that would incapacitate or

00:04:33.200 --> 00:04:36.310
kill humans if ingested.

00:04:36.310 --> 00:04:42.900
So evolution devised a clever way for our
ancestors to identify which foods might be

00:04:42.900 --> 00:04:43.960
toxic.

00:04:43.960 --> 00:04:50.890
We developed tastebuds, thousands of tiny
receptors on our tongue that detect five elements

00:04:50.890 --> 00:04:58.210
of taste: sour, salty, sweet, umami and most
importantly, bitter.

00:04:58.210 --> 00:05:04.070
The toxic substances and poisons found in
certain wild berries, nuts and plants contain

00:05:04.070 --> 00:05:07.290
lots of bitter tasting compounds.

00:05:07.290 --> 00:05:11.760
So if our ancestors put something in their
mouth that tasted bitter there was a chance

00:05:11.760 --> 00:05:13.910
it could be poisonous.

00:05:13.910 --> 00:05:20.310
This was an intelligent and highly effective
system but of course today, we no longer need

00:05:20.310 --> 00:05:22.350
to detect poisons in our food.

00:05:22.350 --> 00:05:27.250
Today food production is highly regulated
in all developed countries, we can simply

00:05:27.250 --> 00:05:30.830
read the packaging to find out exactly what's
in our food.

00:05:30.830 --> 00:05:37.330
But a distaste for bitter foods is still embedded
deep within our DNA.

00:05:37.330 --> 00:05:40.400
We generally find bitterness unpleasant.

00:05:40.400 --> 00:05:44.670
But of course this is not a universal rule
for everyone.

00:05:44.670 --> 00:05:48.510
Some people hate the bitter taste of broccoli,
but many others love it.

00:05:48.510 --> 00:05:53.220
Research has found it all has to do with our
DNA.

00:05:53.220 --> 00:05:58.740
When it comes to bitterness there are two
types of genes, taster genes and non-tasters.

00:05:58.740 --> 00:06:03.890
The taster gene has been passed down from
our hominid ancestors, whereas those with

00:06:03.890 --> 00:06:09.790
the non-taster gene, which is a relatively
recent mutation within the gene-pool, don't

00:06:09.790 --> 00:06:15.350
taste the bitterness in vegetables such as
broccoli and sprouts.

00:06:15.350 --> 00:06:20.669
But of course everyone gets two of these genes,
one from each parent.

00:06:20.669 --> 00:06:26.570
If you get two non-taster genes, one from
each parent, then congratulations, you are

00:06:26.570 --> 00:06:32.669
super lucky because you can't taste the bitterness
in vegetables and other bitter foods.

00:06:32.669 --> 00:06:39.560
But if, on the other hand you got two taster
genes from your parents, then you're most

00:06:39.560 --> 00:06:45.090
likely a very picky eater, especially when
it comes to your greens, because not only

00:06:45.090 --> 00:06:52.120
can you taste bitterness but you're extremely
sensitive to it; to you foods such as broccoli

00:06:52.120 --> 00:06:57.770
will taste almost inedibly bitter, your body
will be repulsed the second it touches your

00:06:57.770 --> 00:06:58.930
tongue.

00:06:58.930 --> 00:07:04.979
So if you know someone who is picky about
their veg, maybe cut them some slack, because

00:07:04.979 --> 00:07:09.780
it's not actually their fault, it's in their
DNA.

00:07:09.780 --> 00:07:16.570
You may be wondering what happens if you get
one gene from each parent, a non taster and

00:07:16.570 --> 00:07:18.350
a taster gene.

00:07:18.350 --> 00:07:22.479
Researchers have found that such people may
not like the taste of bitter foods when they're

00:07:22.479 --> 00:07:29.139
younger but through repeatedly trying such
foods they can actually learn to like them

00:07:29.139 --> 00:07:34.250
as they grow older, and potentially even appreciate
bitter flavours.

00:07:34.250 --> 00:07:39.780
This is why certain foods that you hated when
you were younger are no longer a problem for

00:07:39.780 --> 00:07:42.699
you when you hit your twenties or thirties.

00:07:42.699 --> 00:07:47.410
So that covers the vast majority of people's
food choices.

00:07:47.410 --> 00:07:52.440
But what about the extremely picky eaters,
those individuals that categorically will

00:07:52.440 --> 00:07:57.150
not eat anything but an extremely limited
selection of food.

00:07:57.150 --> 00:08:04.889
You know, that person who will ONLY eat cereal
and nothing else or that person who won't

00:08:04.889 --> 00:08:09.320
eat anything that casts a shadow, oh wait,
that's vegans.

00:08:09.320 --> 00:08:15.400
In such individuals the cause is more than
genetics and we start to enter the realm of

00:08:15.400 --> 00:08:16.400
psychology.

00:08:16.400 --> 00:08:22.410
Doctors believe they have diagnosed such extreme
fussy eaters and it turns out they have a

00:08:22.410 --> 00:08:25.010
genuine eating disorder.

00:08:25.010 --> 00:08:28.160
It's called Food Neophobia.

00:08:28.160 --> 00:08:31.770
We all experience neophobia to some extent.

00:08:31.770 --> 00:08:38.860
It's simply the fear or apprehension of trying
new or unfamiliar foods, but true neophobics

00:08:38.860 --> 00:08:44.199
take that fear to a whole new level, where
even the thought of eating anything except

00:08:44.199 --> 00:08:47.420
what they're used to, make's them feel nauseous.

00:08:47.420 --> 00:08:53.120
It's believed that neophobia has its roots
in genetics, and having certain genes can

00:08:53.120 --> 00:08:56.750
increase a person's neophobic tendencies.

00:08:56.750 --> 00:09:02.139
However to reach a high level of neophobia,
where a person will outright refuse to eat

00:09:02.139 --> 00:09:07.481
anything new, even something that many other
would find desirable, such as pizza, there

00:09:07.481 --> 00:09:12.999
needs to be an added psychosocial factor on
top of their genetics.

00:09:12.999 --> 00:09:17.920
When growing up, if children don't experience
their parents eating a wide variety of foods

00:09:17.920 --> 00:09:23.759
or never trying new foods, they are much more
likely to develop neophobia.

00:09:23.759 --> 00:09:29.059
And of course, parents who are rather unadventurous
with their culinary choices are far more likely

00:09:29.059 --> 00:09:36.519
to give their children uninspired and similar
foods, night after night, which only makes

00:09:36.519 --> 00:09:42.019
the likelihood of the child developing neophobia
far, far worse.

00:09:42.019 --> 00:09:47.800
The biggest problem with neophobia is that
once it sets its claws into a child it's extremely

00:09:47.800 --> 00:09:53.970
hard to break its hold and in the vast majority
of cases it continues into adulthood.

00:09:53.970 --> 00:10:00.259
So how do we prevent neophobia from developing
in the first place?

00:10:00.259 --> 00:10:07.399
Well the answer lies to the east, in a magical
and bizarre place called Japan.

00:10:07.399 --> 00:10:11.699
I've talked previously about how Japan is
the healthiest country in the world, with

00:10:11.699 --> 00:10:14.429
the highest life expectancy.

00:10:14.429 --> 00:10:18.999
It's long been thought that how we are taught
to eat as a child stays with us our whole

00:10:18.999 --> 00:10:25.369
lives and hugely affects our eating habits
as an adult, for better or for worse.

00:10:25.369 --> 00:10:31.089
Japanese culture has taken this to the extreme
and Japanese children eat a far more varied

00:10:31.089 --> 00:10:36.949
and healthier diet than kids in every other
country, which could explain why they have

00:10:36.949 --> 00:10:39.559
such long life expectancies.

00:10:39.559 --> 00:10:45.829
Japanese children have the lowest childhood
obesity rates in the developed world and it's

00:10:45.829 --> 00:10:47.130
easy to see why.

00:10:47.130 --> 00:10:53.509
Snacking between meals is unheard of and Japanese
kids are strongly encouraged to never snack,

00:10:53.509 --> 00:10:55.790
at least until after they've eaten.

00:10:55.790 --> 00:11:01.639
Japanese children eat exactly what their parents
eat, which gets them use to a wide variety

00:11:01.639 --> 00:11:04.679
of foods and cuisines from an early age.

00:11:04.679 --> 00:11:10.329
There's no microwave chicken nuggets for the
kids and then a different meal for the parents

00:11:10.329 --> 00:11:12.980
as is common in many western homes.

00:11:12.980 --> 00:11:16.879
But this approach to food doesn't stop outside
of the home.

00:11:16.879 --> 00:11:22.819
Japanese school dinners are often praised
as being the highest quality and healthiest

00:11:22.819 --> 00:11:25.110
of any country.

00:11:25.110 --> 00:11:29.269
Every meal is cooked from scratch, every morning
in the school kitchen.

00:11:29.269 --> 00:11:31.899
Quality ingredients are sourced from local
farms.

00:11:31.899 --> 00:11:36.980
A typical Japanese school dinner consists
of a few different elements that vary each

00:11:36.980 --> 00:11:44.250
day, but it usually includes tofu and vegetables
in miso soup, rice with seaweed and fish or

00:11:44.250 --> 00:11:46.410
meat with even more vegetables.

00:11:46.410 --> 00:11:54.939
If a kid doesn't like that meal, then tough,
because there is only ever one option each

00:11:54.939 --> 00:11:55.939
day.

00:11:55.939 --> 00:12:01.379
There is no choice at meal times for Japanese
school children; whatever is cooked that day

00:12:01.379 --> 00:12:02.680
is what they will eat.

00:12:02.680 --> 00:12:08.139
Because let's face it, there's no point in
offering a healthy option if 99% of school

00:12:08.139 --> 00:12:11.470
kids are going to instead opt for pizza and
chips.

00:12:11.470 --> 00:12:16.809
But having no other choice works so well in
Japan because there are practically no picky

00:12:16.809 --> 00:12:24.540
eaters, eating disorders such as neophobia
are pretty much non-existent in Japan.

00:12:24.540 --> 00:12:30.240
And maybe that's due to their food culture
within the home and at school.

00:12:30.240 --> 00:12:34.810
So when it comes to eating habits maybe we
should all try to be a little more Japanese.

00:12:34.810 --> 00:12:39.799
Would that be such a bad thing, I mean, they
have ramen; everybody likes ramen.

